{
  "version": 217,
  "released": "2026-08-25",
  "changelog": "v217: people get born and died — person dates become anchors. WHY: birth and death are the two most common datable facts in a life story and they had NO home on a person. `entity_roster._SETTLED_IDENTITY_FIELDS` held only `relationship` and `living`; `entity-verdict` had no date flags; `entity_setup` offered `living: bool` and nothing else. Worse, the landmark set was already COLLECTING two of these dates and dropping them on the floor: the `family` ladder carries a sibling's stated birth year and `family_roster_invocations` never emitted it, and a `losses` entry's person — with their death year, the domain's whole declared unlock (`terminus_ante_quem`) — never reached the roster at all, because the join read only `filed[\"family\"]`. FIX, in one sentence: a roster person carries optional `born` and `died` as full `chronology.DateRecord`s, both in `_SETTLED_IDENTITY_FIELDS` so no refresh can drop them, written by the ONE roster writer and derived into the anchor index exactly once. `entity-verdict --born <EDTF> [--born-basis B] --died <EDTF> [--died-basis B]` reads dates through `chronology.parse_edtf` + the newly PROMOTED `chronology.normalized_date` — the same two calls `landmark-record --date` makes, and `landmarks_interaction._normalized_date` is now an alias of that one body, never a second copy. PRECEDENCE at the store (`entity_verdict._preferred_date`): DERIVED NEVER OVERWRITES STATED, and a same-basis restatement wins by recency — ordered by `chronology.claim_score`, so the strength table is not re-typed. THE TWO DROPPED DATES DIE: `person_roster_invocations` (the pre-v217 name `family_roster_invocations` is kept as an alias of the same function) emits a family member's `--born` with the landmark record's own basis, and `lost_people(landmarks)` sends every `losses` person to the roster with `--died` and `--not-living` through the same `--ensure` mechanics family uses — never page-eligible, so nothing about a loss is published. ANCHORS: `anchors_from_people(people, landmarks)` mints `person:<slug>:born|died` and is wired into `timeline.timeline_data()` and `timeline_interaction.anchors_for_person(people=…)`, which is the `entity_date` unlock `questions.yaml` has declared on `partnerships` and `children` since v197 with zero consumers. ONE ANCHOR PER PERSON PER FACT: when a family (or losses) landmark already anchors the fact, the roster copy is SKIPPED — the landmark store is the source of truth and the roster row is its derived copy, so there is no reconciler and no duplicate. PAGES: a person page's summary opens \"Born 1948, died 2019.\" and its frontmatter carries `born:`/`died:` in EDTF (separate keys, not a reused `date:` span — `timeline.load_entities` reads `date` as a PLACE's residence span, and a lifespan is not that). SCOPE, owner-ruled: person dates as a user feature are FAMILY-ONLY, which is exactly what this builds (family + losses, and a losses row is never page-eligible) — no roster sensitivity field is needed. Phase 2, named and deliberately not done here: `cmd_landmark_record` still hardcodes `basis=\"stated\"`, and `entity_setup` still offers `living: bool` only, so the identity conversation cannot yet volunteer a date.",
